@* THE FORM INSIDE THE OPEN ROW: nine typed fields, the owner's own extra rows, and the save.

   NO form ELEMENT, no EditForm, no validation summary. A bare form with no submit handler turns Enter in any
   text field into a native GET submit, which reloads the whole WebAssembly app and loses every unsaved edit.
   A validation summary is an error summary at the top of the form, which this project's rules forbid outright
   for a ten-field form inside a list row: it forces a second journey back down. Every button therefore carries
   an explicit type of button, because the HTML default is submit, and every error is reached only through the
   input's own aria-describedby. FacetPanel.razor is the house precedent.

   THE OVERRIDE TRIPLE is the hardest thing on this page: a checkbox, the input, and a sentence saying which
   state the field is in. With CSS off the three states are still told apart, because the sentence says which
   one it is in words. Never a placeholder showing the pipeline's value (a placeholder is not a value, has no
   contrast guarantee, and vanishes on the first keystroke), and never a fade or a muted class: opacity turns
   the contrast scan INCOMPLETE, which is a build break here, and the muted class ships !important.
   THE UNSET SENTENCE NAMES THE RESOLVED VALUE, because the directors, the Apple genre and the purchase
   information appear nowhere else on this screen -- the row's own toggle reads the band, the title, the year
   and the runtime and nothing more.

   THE RUNTIME IS WRITTEN WHETHER THE BOX IS TICKED OR NOT, and its unchecked sentence says so rather than
   claiming the pipeline decides. RecordLink.PublishedKeyOf falls back to the sheet echo's runtime and
   RuntimeParser.FilmMinutes throws on a bare minute count, so a record saved without one becomes unplaceable:
   the correction would be in the file and bound to nothing. The same fact is why the form's "overrides
   nothing" judgment ignores the runtime.

   NOTHING IS MADE INERT. An input that cannot be edited would be readonly and never disabled or merely
   aria-disabled -- disabled drops focus if the element has it, and aria-disabled on a text input does not stop
   typing, so it would lie. But readonly has no effect on a checkbox, so a read-only version of THIS form
   cannot be built at all: where nothing is editable the panel renders no inputs and no Save control, the
   sentences stay as prose, and one sentence says why. There is nothing to make inert and no fake affordance.

   NO KEY HANDLER, and no Escape to close. The disclosure pattern was chosen precisely so there is no
   keyboard-trap obligation to discharge, and an Escape that threw away unsaved edits would be a new silent
   loss. Closing is the row's own Close control, which already returns focus to the row's toggle.

   THE OUTCOME CONTAINER IS THE SAME ELEMENT IN EVERY STATE and is the form's own, not the page's. Every save
   outcome, success and failure alike, is reported by moving focus to it: the worklist owns the page's one
   polite region for its debounced count, and the page's load-state container renders ABOVE the worklist, so
   focusing that from row 200 of 285 would throw focus to the top of the page. No live-region attribute and no
   status or alert role appears anywhere in this file; a focused container's own text is the statement. *@

<div class="record-form">
    @if (!Editable)
    {
        <p class="record-locked">@NotEditableSentence</p>

        @foreach (var field in Draft.Fields)
        {
            <p class="record-told"><span class="record-told-name">@field.Label</span> @field.Describe()</p>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        @foreach (var group in Groups)
        {
            <fieldset>
                <legend>@group.Legend</legend>

                @foreach (var (slug, field) in group.Fields)
                {
                    <div class="record-field">
                        @* A wrapping label needs no for attribute, and the checkbox's words are not the
                           input's: two controls sharing one accessible name is not navigable. *@
                        <label class="record-toggle">
                            <input type="checkbox" id="@IdFor(slug, "override")" checked="@field.Overridden"
                                   @onchange="e => Toggle(field, e)" />
                            @field.OverrideLabel
                        </label>

                        <label for="@IdFor(slug)">@field.Label</label>
                        @* Integer fields are text with a numeric input mode and never type=number: spinner
                           arrows are well under the target-size floor and adjacent, the scroll wheel changes
                           the value silently, and the role is announced as "spin button" for what is a plain
                           id. Nothing carries pattern, min, max, step, maxlength or required either -- each
                           turns on native constraint validation, whose bubble is a second error channel with
                           no aria-describedby link. The validation is in C#. *@
                        <input id="@IdFor(slug)" type="text" value="@field.Typed"
                               inputmode="@(field.Shape == FieldShape.Integer ? "numeric" : null)"
                               aria-invalid="@(Draft.ShowProblem(field) ? "true" : null)"
                               aria-describedby="@DescribedBy(slug, field)"
                               @oninput="e => Type(field, e)" @onblur="() => Leave(field)"
                               @ref="Slot(IdFor(slug)).Element" />

                        <p id="@IdFor(slug, "state")" class="record-field-state">@field.Describe()</p>

                        @if (field.Shape == FieldShape.Candidates)
                        {
                            @* The format BEFORE the attempt, in a third described-by id. *@
                            <p id="@IdFor(slug, "format")" class="record-field-format">@FieldDraft.ListShape</p>
                        }

                        @* Immediately after its input, and in the described-by list only while it exists: an
                           always-rendered empty paragraph is a resolve target with no text. *@
                        @if (Draft.ShowProblem(field))
                        {
                            <p id="@IdFor(slug, "error")" class="record-field-error">@Draft.ProblemFor(field)</p>
                        }
                    </div>
                }

                @if (group.Chooser && Draft.Offered.Count > 0)
                {
                    @* THE SHORTLIST IS A RADIO GROUP AND NOT A ROW OF BUTTONS. Choosing a candidate may not
                       move focus, so with buttons focus would stay where it was while the id field and both
                       state sentences changed with nothing to announce them, and this page's one polite
                       region belongs to the worklist. A radio's checked state is the announcement, natively.
                       It also makes the id/candidates exclusion structural rather than a rule to remember,
                       and it keeps the undecided shortlist, which is the whole point of offering it. *@
                    <fieldset class="record-chooser">
                        <legend>Candidate TMDB ids</legend>
                        <div class="record-options">
                            @foreach (var offered in Draft.Offered)
                            {
                                var candidate = offered;
                                <label>
                                    <input type="radio" id="@IdFor("candidate", Spell(candidate))"
                                           name="@IdFor("candidate")" checked="@Draft.Chose(candidate)"
                                           @onchange="() => Draft.Choose(candidate)" />
                                    Use @Spell(candidate)
                                </label>
                            }
                            <label>
                                <input type="radio" id="@IdFor("candidate-undecided")" name="@IdFor("candidate")"
                                       checked="@Draft.Undecided" @onchange="() => Draft.Choose(null)" />
                                Keep all of these as candidates — undecided
                            </label>
                        </div>
                    </fieldset>
                }
            </fieldset>
        }

        @* EACH ROW IS ITS OWN GROUP AND ITS OWN LABELS, both halves and not either. A legend names the group
           and is not part of an input's accessible name, so a legend alone leaves twelve inputs all called
           "Field name". The key is the row's synthetic id: the loop index shifts on a remove and the typed
           name changes on every keystroke, and either makes Blazor destroy the input he is typing in. *@
        <fieldset>
            <legend>Extra fields</legend>

            @for (var i = 0; i < Draft.Extras.Count; i++)
            {
                var position = Spell(i + 1);
                var row = Draft.Extras[i];
                var nameId = IdFor($"extra-{position}-name");
                var valueId = IdFor($"extra-{position}-value");
                var errorId = IdFor($"extra-{position}-error");

                <fieldset class="record-extra" @key="row.Id">
                    <legend>Extra field @position</legend>

                    <div class="record-field">
                        <label for="@nameId">Field @position name</label>
                        <input id="@nameId" type="text" value="@row.Name" autocomplete="off"
                               aria-invalid="@(Draft.ShowProblem(row) ? "true" : null)"
                               aria-describedby="@(Draft.ShowProblem(row) ? errorId : null)"
                               @oninput="e => Name(row, e)" @onblur="() => Leave(row)"
                               @ref="Slot(ExtraSlot(row)).Element" />

                        @if (Draft.ShowProblem(row))
                        {
                            <p id="@errorId" class="record-field-error">@Draft.ProblemFor(row)</p>
                        }
                    </div>

                    <div class="record-field">
                        <label for="@valueId">Field @position value</label>
                        <input id="@valueId" type="text" value="@row.Value" autocomplete="off"
                               @oninput="e => Value(row, e)" />
                    </div>

                    @* The position comes first, so the name is stable while he types the key and still unique
                       when two rows hold the same text, and the name CONTAINS the visible word verbatim. *@
                    <button type="button" class="record-remove" aria-label="@RemoveName(position, row)"
                            @onclick="() => Remove(row)">Remove</button>
                </fieldset>
            }

            <button type="button" class="record-add" @ref="addButton" @onclick="Add">Add a field</button>
        </fieldset>

        @* WHICH MECHANISM WILL BE USED IS SAID IN TEXT BEFORE HE PRESSES ANYTHING, and when the mechanism
           differs the control's NAME differs: one name for two behaviours tells him one thing and gives him
           another. The folder button stays in the DOM once a folder is granted, because the refusal message
           ends "Choose the folder again, then retry" and hiding the control points that instruction at
           nothing; it is not rendered at all where there is no folder API to ask, because then it is an
           instruction he cannot follow for a control that would do nothing. *@
        <div class="record-save-area">
            @if (ApiSupported)
            {
                @* A54: focus comes back here whichever way the prompt ended, and the granted state is this
                   button's own description -- so the arrival speaks "Saving to: MovieLibrary" or "No folder
                   chosen yet", with no live region and nothing announced twice. *@
                <button type="button" class="record-folder" aria-describedby="@IdFor("grant")"
                        @onclick="ChooseFolder" @ref="folderButton">
                    Choose the folder to save corrections to
                </button>
            }

            <p id="@IdFor("grant")" class="record-grant">@GrantSentence</p>
            <p id="@IdFor("backup")" class="record-backup">@BackupSentence</p>

            @* aria-disabled from a string and never the disabled attribute: disabling the focused element
               blurs it and drops focus to the body. The click still arrives and is ignored in code, with no
               report and no focus move, because the control has already said it is unavailable and its
               description already says why. It keeps its boundary in that state: with a transparent
               background the border is the control's only edge. *@
            <button type="button" class="record-save" aria-disabled="@(Available ? null : "true")"
                    aria-describedby="@SaveDescribedBy" @onclick="SaveClick">@SaveName</button>
        </div>
    }

    @* ONE STABLE CONTAINER, never swapped for a different node: replacing it would destroy the node a screen
       reader's virtual cursor may be sitting on. It is rendered in every state, including the one with no
       controls at all, and it is the only thing any save outcome is reported through. *@
    <p class="record-state" tabindex="-1" @ref="outcome">@Outcome</p>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Why nothing can be changed, where nothing can be. It names the page's own container rather than
    /// repeating its sentence: that sentence is a load state, it is already on screen above the list, and two
    /// copies of one reason are two things to keep in step.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string NotEditableSentence =
        "This record cannot be corrected, for the reason given at the top of the page. The values below are "
        + "what the site shows.";

    /// <summary>Said before he presses anything, and the reason the control's name differs too.</summary>
    internal const string DownloadMechanismSentence =
        "This browser cannot write to a folder. Save will download the file to your Downloads folder instead.";

    /// <summary>A53. Deliberately NOT rendered where there is no folder API: it would be unfollowable.</summary>
    internal const string NoFolderSentence = "No folder chosen yet. Choose a folder before saving.";

    /// <summary>A58, in the one state where the file can keep the promise: the previous version, never the original.</summary>
    internal const string BackupPromise =
        "Each save copies the current record-store.json to record-store.json.bak first, so the previous "
        + "version is one step back.";

    /// <summary>
    /// M8. The script writes the backup only when the existing file was READ, so a fresh clone's first save
    /// creates the file with no backup at all, and promising one here would be a promise the file cannot keep.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string NoBackupYetSentence =
        "No record store was read, so this save may be creating record-store.json for the first time and "
        + "there is no previous version to keep.";

    /// <summary>
    /// The download route makes no backup claim at all. What stands in its place is the checked half: the
    /// store is read back through the reader the next build uses before anything leaves the page.
    /// </summary>
    internal const string DownloadNoBackupSentence =
        "Nothing is written to the repository folder on this route, so there is no previous version to keep. "
        + "What stands in its place is the check before the save: the file is read back through the same "
        + "reader the next build will use.";

    internal const string SaveToFolderName = "Save to folder";

    internal const string DownloadName = "Download corrections file";

    /// <summary>The row this form corrects. Its published key scopes every id in here.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required WorklistRow Row { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The draft being edited, which the PAGE owns. The worklist closes the open row on every debounced query
    /// change, so a draft held here would be destroyed 150 ms after one character reached the search box.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public required RecordDraft Draft { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a correction may be made at all. False renders no inputs and no Save control.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Editable { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Whether this browser can write to a folder. False is the download route.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool ApiSupported { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The granted folder's name, or null when none has been granted in this browser.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? FolderName { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What reading the store produced, which decides whether a backup can be promised.</summary>
    [Parameter] public StoreReading Store { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a save is in flight. The control says so and the click is ignored.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Saving { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The one sentence in the outcome container. Empty renders an empty container, not no container.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Outcome { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>Save was pressed and the control was available. The page does the writing.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback Save { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The folder button was pressed. The page asks the browser, because the grant is the page's.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback ChooseFolder { get; set; }

    private sealed record Group(string Legend, (string Slug, FieldDraft Field)[] Fields, bool Chooser = false);

    /// <summary>
    /// The five named groups, in render order. The count is deliberately not fixed: each extras row is a
    /// fieldset of its own and the chooser adds another, so what is asserted is that every group has a legend.
    /// </summary>
    private Group[] Groups =>
    [
        new("Match", [("tmdb-id", Draft.TmdbId), ("candidates", Draft.Candidates)], Chooser: true),
        new("Titles", [("display-title", Draft.DisplayTitle), ("sort-title", Draft.SortTitle)]),
        new("Facts", [("year", Draft.Year), ("runtime", Draft.Runtime), ("directors", Draft.Directors)]),
        new("Source", [("apple-genre", Draft.AppleGenre), ("purchase-info", Draft.PurchaseInfo)]),
    ];

    private sealed class RefSlot { public ElementReference Element; }

    private readonly Dictionary<string, RefSlot> slots = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    private ElementReference outcome;
    private ElementReference addButton;
    private ElementReference folderButton;
    private string? pendingFocus;
    private bool focusAdd;

    /// <summary>
    /// EVERY ID IS ROW-SCOPED, matching the worklist's own row-{k}-fields. One row is open at a time today,
    /// but a global id breaks the moment two renders overlap, and a matched record's key begins with a digit,
    /// which is why the prefix is a letter: an id starting with a digit is not a valid bare CSS selector.
    /// </summary>
    private string IdFor(string slug) => $"row-{Row.Movie.K}-{slug}";

    private string IdFor(string slug, string part) => $"row-{Row.Movie.K}-{slug}-{part}";

    /// <summary>
    /// Built conditionally, because every id in the list has to resolve in the same render: the state
    /// sentence always, the format instruction where the field takes one, the error only while it exists.
    /// </summary>
    private string DescribedBy(string slug, FieldDraft field)
    {
        var ids = IdFor(slug, "state");

        if (field.Shape == FieldShape.Candidates) ids += " " + IdFor(slug, "format");
        if (Draft.ShowProblem(field)) ids += " " + IdFor(slug, "error");

        return ids;
    }

    private string SaveDescribedBy => $"{IdFor("grant")} {IdFor("backup")}";

    /// <summary>A53 and A56: what will happen, in visible text, and part of the Save control's description.</summary>
    private string GrantSentence => !ApiSupported
        ? DownloadMechanismSentence
        : FolderName is { } folder ? $"Saving to: {folder}" : NoFolderSentence;

    private string BackupSentence => !ApiSupported
        ? DownloadNoBackupSentence
        : Store == StoreReading.Read ? BackupPromise : NoBackupYetSentence;

    private string SaveName => ApiSupported ? SaveToFolderName : DownloadName;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether Save does anything. Unavailable before a folder is granted and while a write is in flight: the
    /// script copies the CURRENT file to the backup on every write, so a second press would make the second
    /// save's backup the first save's output and "one step back" would silently become a state he never had.
    /// </summary>
    private bool Available => Editable && !Saving && (!ApiSupported || FolderName is not null);

    /// <summary>
    /// A row's reference slot is keyed on its SYNTHETIC id and never on its position. A reference capture is
    /// applied only on the render that creates the element, so a slot keyed on the position would still hold
    /// the reference of the element that USED to be at that position -- and after a remove the focus move
    /// would go to an element that is no longer in the document, which drops focus to the body: the exact
    /// failure A30 exists to prevent, arrived at by obeying it.
    /// </summary>
    private static string ExtraSlot(ExtraRow row) => $"extra-row-{Spell(row.Id)}";

    private RefSlot Slot(string id)
    {
        if (!slots.TryGetValue(id, out var slot)) slots[id] = slot = new RefSlot();

        return slot;
    }

    private static string Spell(int value) => value.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);

    private static string RemoveName(string position, ExtraRow row) =>
        row.Name.Trim() is { Length: > 0 } name
            ? $"Remove field {position}, \"{name}\""
            : $"Remove field {position}";

    // ============================================================ the edits

    private static void Toggle(FieldDraft field, ChangeEventArgs e) => field.Overridden = e.Value is true;

    private static void Type(FieldDraft field, ChangeEventArgs e) => field.Typed = e.Value as string ?? "";

    private static void Name(ExtraRow row, ChangeEventArgs e) => row.Name = e.Value as string ?? "";

    private static void Value(ExtraRow row, ChangeEventArgs e) => row.Value = e.Value as string ?? "";

    // Errors appear when a field is LEFT, and on Save, and never per keystroke: an invalid flag flipping on
    // every character re-announces the field as he types, and a freshly ticked override would announce an
    // error he has not made yet. Marking a field on blur neither saves nor moves focus, so nothing here is a
    // change of context.
    private static void Leave(FieldDraft field) => field.Left = true;

    private static void Leave(ExtraRow row) => row.Left = true;

    /// <summary>
    /// Focus moves into the new row, or the add is invisible: it appears below the control he pressed with
    /// nothing to tell him so. Its own label is then read as focus arrives, which IS the statement -- nothing
    /// further is said, or one action would be stated twice.
    /// </summary>
    private void Add() => pendingFocus = ExtraSlot(Draft.AddExtra());

    /// <summary>
    /// The pressed control is destroyed by the action it performs, so focus goes to the next row's name, or
    /// to the Add button when the removed row was the last one. Focus on a removed element falls to the body,
    /// and the next Tab restarts above the skip link.
    /// </summary>
    private void Remove(ExtraRow row)
    {
        if (!Draft.Extras.Contains(row)) return;

        var next = Draft.RemoveExtra(row);
        pendingFocus = next is null ? null : ExtraSlot(next);
        focusAdd = next is null;
    }

    private async Task SaveClick()
    {
        // The control already says it is unavailable and its description already says why, so the ignored
        // click produces no report and no focus move.
        if (!Available) return;

        // "Save has been pressed" is a fact about this control, so it is recorded here: it is what turns the
        // errors on for fields he never visited.
        Draft.SaveAttempted = true;
        await Save.InvokeAsync();
    }

    // ============================================================ focus

    /// <remarks>
    /// After the render that carries the new state and never from the handler: a target that does not exist
    /// yet cannot be focused, and the element the add created is only there once the batch has been applied.
    /// </remarks>
    protected override async Task OnAfterRenderAsync(bool firstRender)
    {
        if (focusAdd)
        {
            focusAdd = false;
            await TryFocus(addButton);
            return;
        }

        if (pendingFocus is { } id)
        {
            pendingFocus = null;
            await TryFocus(Slot(id).Element);
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Moves focus to the outcome container, whose own text is the report. True if the move happened.</summary>
    public Task<bool> FocusOutcomeAsync() => TryFocus(outcome);

    /// <summary>
    /// A54. Back to the control that opened the directory prompt, whichever way it ended. The outcome is not
    /// announced from here: this button's description IS the granted state, so the arrival states it.
    /// </summary>
    public Task<bool> FocusFolderAsync() => TryFocus(folderButton);

    /// <summary>
    /// Moves focus to the first field that is wrong, in render order, whose error is then read as its
    /// description. No summary at the top: for a ten-field form inside a list row that forces a second
    /// journey back down.
    /// </summary>
    public async Task<bool> FocusFirstProblemAsync()
    {
        foreach (var (slug, field) in Groups.SelectMany(g => g.Fields))
            if (Draft.ProblemFor(field) is not null)
                return await TryFocus(Slot(IdFor(slug)).Element);

        foreach (var row in Draft.Extras)
            if (Draft.ProblemFor(row) is not null)
                return await TryFocus(Slot(ExtraSlot(row)).Element);

        return false;
    }

    /// <remarks>
    /// Interop can fail, and an escape from here would reveal the host page's error bar, which carries an
    /// assertive role of its own: two spoken statements for one event. The answer says whether the move
    /// happened, because the assertive fallback is raised only where it did not.
    /// </remarks>
    private static async Task<bool> TryFocus(ElementReference element)
    {
        try
        {
            await element.FocusAsync();
            return true;
        }
        catch (Exception ex) when (ex is JSException or TaskCanceledException or ObjectDisposedException)
        {
            return false;
        }
    }
}
